  text="A rose-colored meteorite (a rock that fell to Earth from space) found in the Sahara in Mali in 2022, named Teghaza 001, formed on Mars more than 4.1 billion years ago, according to a study published 29 October in the journal Science. That makes it one of the three oldest of the roughly 435 Martian meteorites known; the others are ALH84001, found in Antarctica in 1984, which is of similar age, and \"Black Beauty\". Water altered the rock about 4.1 billion years ago, so it formed even earlier, and the team says this supports the idea that early Mars had liquid water and conditions that could have suited life, though it does not show that life existed. It is a single kind of coarse igneous rock (gabbro, which cooled slowly from molten rock below the surface), unlike Black Beauty, which is a jumble of many rock pieces. Its minerals best match Mars's ancient southern highlands as seen from orbit by NASA's Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter (MRO); NASA shows 13 possible source spots on a MOLA map (an elevation map made by a laser altimeter on the old Mars Global Surveyor orbiter). The lead author is Yang Liu of JPL; the University of New Mexico first spotted that it came from Mars from its oxygen isotopes (different forms of the oxygen atom).",
